# Verification brief for {{ ticket.key }}

The tests and the implementation are both promoted, and this workspace holds
both — the promoted versions, whatever either stage did to its own copy.

Run the suite and write down what happened.

## Output

Write `{{ report_path }}`:

```json
{
  "outcome": "GREEN",
  "tests": [
    {"id": "tests/test_reset.py::test_link_expires", "status": "passed"},
    {"id": "tests/test_reset.py::test_expired_refused", "status": "failed",
     "failure_kind": "assertion"}
  ]
}
```

`outcome` is `GREEN`, `RED`, or `UNCOLLECTABLE` — the last one when the suite
could not even be collected, which is a different problem from a failing test.

`failure_kind` is `assertion`, `import`, or `error`.

## Your outcome is checked against a real run

lazyfish runs this repository's configured test command in this workspace and
keeps its exit code. If your report says GREEN and the command fails, or says
RED and it passes, the report is rejected.

The per-test detail is not checked. It is read by people and by the review that
follows, so it is worth getting right for reasons other than passing a check.

## Red is a legitimate outcome

This stage reports; it does not judge. A suite that fails is a fact worth
recording accurately, and recording it is what lets the failure be acted on.
Do not adjust the tests or the implementation to make a nicer report — neither
edit would travel anyway, and the report would then be false as well.
